# An RFC 4180 reader, as a table.
#
# Four states and four kinds read a byte stream one byte at a time. `FieldStart` is where a
# field begins, `Unquoted` is inside a bare field, `Quoted` is inside a quoted one, and
# `QuoteSeen` is the instant after a quote that may be closing a field or escaping another.
# The driver (csv.cpp) classifies each byte into one of the four kinds and carries the byte
# itself on the event — that is what lets one `append` action serve every row that collects a
# character, the way the calculator carries an operand.
#
# The asymmetry is the machine's whole argument. Inside `Quoted`, a comma and a line end are
# DATA — the reason quoting exists at all — and the same two byte kinds end things in every
# other state. One table, and the difference between "a,b" and "a,b" falls out of which state
# the byte arrived in.
#
# The one row MISSING on purpose is `QuoteSeen --data-->`. A byte after a closing quote is not
# RFC 4180, and the format's refusal — no row — IS the error handling: process() returns false,
# the driver reports the byte and stops, and nothing invents a transition to absorb it
# (README.md, "The refusal").
#
# Two rows do more than their name says, and both are the format's boundary rather than a
# taste. `end_record` closes the pending field AND the record, because a line end does two
# things and a row has one action slot (QUESTIONS.md Q24). Nothing here is guarded: no row
# compares a value, so the four kinds and the four states are the whole of what the machine
# can say.
version 1
machine Csv
initial FieldStart

kind data = 1
kind comma = 2
kind quote = 3
kind newline = 4

# FieldStart: a field is about to begin. A quote opens one; a comma ends an empty one in place;
# a line end ends an empty last field and the record; anything else is the field's first byte.
transition FieldStart --data--> Unquoted action append
transition FieldStart --quote--> Quoted
transition FieldStart --comma--> FieldStart action end_cell
transition FieldStart --newline--> FieldStart action end_record

# Unquoted: a bare field. A quote here is malformed RFC 4180 and is tolerated and appended
# rather than refused, which is a reading this repository took rather than a rule the format
# gave (QUESTIONS.md Q22). A comma or a line end ends the field.
transition Unquoted --data--> Unquoted action append
transition Unquoted --quote--> Unquoted action append
transition Unquoted --comma--> FieldStart action end_cell
transition Unquoted --newline--> FieldStart action end_record

# Quoted: a quoted field. Every byte kind is data here — the comma and the line end included,
# which is what the opening quote bought. A quote may be the field's end or the first half of
# an escaped quote, so it is `QuoteSeen`'s to decide.
transition Quoted --data--> Quoted action append
transition Quoted --comma--> Quoted action append
transition Quoted --quote--> QuoteSeen
transition Quoted --newline--> Quoted action append

# QuoteSeen: a quote was just read. Another quote is the escape `""`, and it is appended as one
# quote; a comma or a line end closes the field. A data byte has no row here at all.
transition QuoteSeen --quote--> Quoted action append
transition QuoteSeen --comma--> FieldStart action end_cell
transition QuoteSeen --newline--> FieldStart action end_record
